/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  alpha01_water_methanol_psat --- DOES A PER-COMPONENT ALPHA FUNCTION FIX THE
  CUBIC'S OWN VAPOUR PRESSURE?  (DEV.md 4c C408, slice 0; design record
  docs/design/c408-eos-ge-mixing-rules.md, finding F2 and ruling 2.)

  THE PROBLEM.  SRK with the engine's generalised (Graboski-Daubert) Soave
  slope misses the normal-boiling vapour pressure by -8.8 % for water and
  -2.7 % for methanol.  A mixing rule built on that cubic inherits the error
  at both ends of the diagram, whatever the rule.

  THE REMEDY, OPT-IN.  `alphaFunction MathiasCopeman;` on the cubic replaces
  alpha = [1 + m(1 - sqrt Tr)]^2 by
      alpha = [1 + c1 x + c2 x^2 + c3 x^3]^2,  x = 1 - sqrt(Tr)   (Tr <= 1)
  (Poling, Prausnitz & O'Connell, 5th ed., Table 4-7, pp. 4.19-4.20; above Tc
  the Soave branch with c1 only).  c1..c3 are NOT typed from a table: they are
  fitted BY CURATION (bin/curate/fit_alpha_function.py) to each record's OWN
  vapour-pressure law over the window that law declares, and stored in the
  record (`cubicAlpha { SRK { ... } }`, provenance origin `derived`, with the
  law, the window, the largest deviation and a value sha).  With c2 = c3 = 0
  the form IS Soave's, so a record without the block is today's behaviour.

  WHAT EACH OPERATION PRINTS.  For the pure liquid, `P_bubble` is the CUBIC's
  saturation pressure at T (the incipient vapour is the same pure fluid) and
  `Psat_<component>` is the record's own Antoine law, read from the same
  package.  The ratio is the deviation.  Nine to eleven temperatures span the law's
  declared window (water 293-373 K of its 273-373 K -- below 293 K the pure
  liquid's pressure is under the 0.01 bar floor of the bubble-pressure solver,
  which refuses it by name -- and methanol's whole 288-356 K); the `_Tb` points sit
  at the normal boiling point.

  ANCHORS.  The `_Tb` rows of the Mathias-Copeman operations carry an anchor:
  at the normal boiling point the saturation pressure is 101325 Pa BY
  DEFINITION.  Water is read at 373.1243 K, the temperature at which IAPWS
  SR1-86 Table 1 tabulates p = 101325 Pa (quoted in data/standards/components/
  water.dat's header); methanol at the record's Tb = 337.85 K.  The anchor
  tolerances (3e-2 and 1e-2) are the in-sample deviation of the SAVED records
  plus margin: water's Antoine law is itself 0.1-2.5 % off IAPWS across its
  window (its header says so), and the Mathias-Copeman constants reproduce
  THAT law, not the primary -- which is the honest statement of what curating
  to the record's own law buys.

  WHAT THIS DOES NOT SAY.  It does not say the constants are right outside the
  window: above it they extrapolate, and CO2 is not curated at all -- its
  Antoine law is the SUBLIMATION line and the tool refuses it by name.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
operations
(
    {
        name        water_soave_window;
        type        propertyScan1D;
        vary        { variable T;  from 293 K;  to 373 K;  n 9; }
        state       { P 1.01325 bar;  composition { water 1.0; methanol 0.0; } }
        thermo
        {
            equilibrium
            {
                formulation phiPhi;
                equationOfState { model SRK; }
                liquid { fugacityRoute equationOfState; root liquid; }
                vapour { fugacityRoute equationOfState; root vapour; }
            }
            caloric
            {
                energyBasis elementsDatum;
                liquid { departureRoute equilibriumEquationOfState; root liquid; }
                vapour { departureRoute equilibriumEquationOfState; root vapour; }
            }
        }
        properties  ( P_bubble  Psat_water );
        output      { file water_soave.csv; }
    }
    {
        name        water_soave_Tb;
